Mojo native test coverage audit (2026-10-09)
=========================================

Verified combined result
------------------------
The full native CPU rerun passed 1169 Bazel test targets, with six skipped
targets and no failures (205 more passing targets than the original CPU
selection). Nine additional documentation targets and the native LSP target
also passed in subsequent runs: 1179 passing targets across these runs, with
the same six top-level skips. Six configuration unittest cases pass separately. The port's Makefile.tests now includes the additional
native targets: all 35 applicable Support unit suites, all eight AsyncRT unit
suites, 155 documentation examples, documentation compiler and Support
regressions, the build-ID shared-library test, and small example/tool tests.
FreeBSD OS detection and socket layouts have new native tests. The 96 CLI cases
and 20 documentation-generator cases pass; the 208 integration cases have
207 passes and one expected failure. See the sections below for remaining
coverage gaps, including skips inside passing targets and missing AI runtimes.

Scope
-----
The earlier 964-pass/6-skip result was the selected native CPU suite, not
all upstream tests. Inventory covers Mojo, Support and AsyncRT at the pinned
source revision. Bazel-generated per-file lit targets and aggregate targets
often execute the same cases; they must not be added as distinct coverage.
Manual clang-tidy, documentation lint and Python dependency checks are recorded
separately from runtime/compiler regression tests. MAX's full product test tree
and LLVM's own complete regression suite have not been qualified.

Gaps found and exercised
-----------------------
Only five of the 36 Support C++ unit targets had been selected. The Linux-only
NIXL fixture is not a native-port requirement; the other 35 can run here.
All eight AsyncRT unit targets were missing. These cover allocators, async
values, CPU devices, debugging allocators, timers, type IDs, work queues and
worker signal stacks. The teaching example's increment/decrement tests were
also omitted.

The Support compiler regression aggregate passes 26 cases and skips one
macOS-only case. Its native target omits an unused Crashpad tool dependency.
The isolated Tracy bridge test also passes, without testing a MAX trace context.

The port lacked a FreeBSD OS-predicate test and FreeBSD socket ABI mirrors.
The new ABI test uses generated C compiled against local sys/socket.h,
netinet/in.h and sys/un.h to check five sockaddr types, sa_family_t and
SO_NOSIGPIPE, including field types/offsets, size and alignment. This closes
layout coverage, not end-to-end socket I/O qualification.

The original CLI selection omitted 17 of 102 upstream cases. Eleven additional
cases (eight doc commands plus driver/cache and two missing-main diagnostics)
need neither MAX nor debugger/formatter tools. The documentation compiler has
21 regression files; 20 can run without the MAX-dependent mojo_doc.mojo.
Three KGEN ELF/CPU tests, one CLI code-model test, and one runtime
object-emission integration test had
Linux-only requirements despite being applicable to the qualified FreeBSD
amd64 host. Their original output checks are retained.

The shared-library build-ID regression exposed two problems: a hard-coded
/bin/bash interpreter, then symbol interposition making the shared library
report the executable's ID. FreeBSD now uses the upstream hidden visibility
setting for Support:BinaryID. BufferTest and ContextTest require enabled inline
C++ assertions; compile those test translation units with -UNDEBUG, like the
existing AssertStreamTest. Do not remove their death checks.

The documentation C FFI clock example also lacked FreeBSD CLOCK_MONOTONIC=4;
the native header value was added to the example and its test.

An additional staged-package Python run passes 93 cases with no skips or
failures: eight importer/path cases and 85 cases across all 12 extension
example groups. These are separate from the 1169 Bazel target count.
The reusable target is make test-python-package in lang/mojo. It builds
upstream extension fixtures with the staged compiler and runs their original
pytest checks. The verified environment used Python 3.12, pytest 8.2.2 and
NumPy 2.4.6; newer pytest versions selected by ports are not yet qualified.
The launcher imports sys into __main__, as the upstream launcher does, before
pytest starts; omitting that setup caused earlier fixture failures. Original
failure logs and the corrected results are retained under
/usr/obj/mojo-port-validation/2026-10-09/python-package-target/ and
python-harness/. Bazel's FreeBSD pycross dependency selection is still open.

Remaining coverage and capability gaps
--------------------------------------
* Python: the two mojo Python unit suites and all 12 extension example
  suites now pass through the staged-package target (93 cases; see above).
  Bazel pytest/pycross still lacks native FreeBSD dependency selection.
  All six Support configuration cases now pass through native unittest and
  are included in do-test. The Nanobind test rule aliases a prebuilt MAX
  wheel in the upstream public build; its native extension remains unqualified.
* Documentation examples: 165 runtime test targets were discovered, plus a
  Python dependency check. All 161 applicable non-manual CPU examples now pass.
  Six newly selected examples no longer inherit an unused package-wide MAX
  dependency; comptime_subexpression.mojo keeps its actual layout dependency.
  Three notebook targets are GPU-tagged. All three additional manual decorator
  examples also pass and are explicitly selected. The nine new examples passed
  both direct compilation/execution and their original Bazel targets.
  Evidence: additional-cpu-docs-final/ and additional-doc-bazel-tests.log.
  Native NumPy is represented
  by a Python provider wrapper, using the same ports test dependency as the
  standard-library NumPy tests. MAX/GPU examples cannot inherit CPU qualification.
* Tooling: native LSP passes 12 regression cases; definition.test is disabled
  upstream (REQUIRES: disabled). The language server is now included in the
  port. The upstream parse/semantic-check scan also passes all 259 stdlib files;
  this does not cover docstring code-block checking. A fresh package unpack
  passes the LSP version and original initialization protocol checks. The
  upstream-disabled definition test was checked directly and still fails its
  original expectations.
  Evidence: native-lsp/. LLDB/debugger, REPL, Jupyter and JavaScript LSP remain
  unqualified. The isolated VariantTypeNameParser unit test passes; this does
  not qualify debugger execution. The JavaScript package cannot load because aspect_rules_js asks
  for an unavailable yq_freebsd_amd64 repository. Its pinned rules_nodejs
  toolchain table also omits FreeBSD, and the Mocha environment depends on
  MAX extensibility (a hover case imports Layout). www/node24 exists in ports,
  but no native Node executable is installed on this validation host. These
  are distinct harness/runtime gaps, not native LSP test failures. One Jupyter completion test is
  excluded upstream for a known crash; debugger tests also contain upstream
  disabled cases. The formatter's regular suite now passes 1004 cases with two
  upstream skips, and all three original formatter CLI tests pass. Optional
  source-compilation validation finished with 907 passes, 25 failures and four
  skips. Failures include incomplete or unsupported source snippets; tests/util.py explicitly documents that many
  formatter samples cannot compile in isolation. These failures are retained,
  not counted as formatting-suite passes. Dependency/lint checks remain separate.
* Runtime update: native FreeBSD domain CPU discovery and thread CPU/memory
  affinity now pass all 18 WorkQueue cases, including six previously skipped
  NUMA cases and two previously Linux-only CPU-placement cases. BaseTest passes
  40 cases, including two new checks for affinity/policy restoration and invalid
  CPU handling. The host has vm.ndomains=1; physical multi-domain placement,
  PCI locality and Linux-only allocation-placement bodies remain unqualified.
  Evidence: /usr/obj/mojo-port-validation/2026-10-09/native-numa/.
  The full CPU rerun with the native Bazel executable also passed: 1169 targets
  pass and six skip, with 1120 targets executed. The result includes these
  runtime changes. Evidence: native-cpu-final/ under the validation directory.
* Telemetry: five tests are disabled upstream. Active tests use local files or
  mocks; their success does not cover crash reporting. Crashpad integration is
  unavailable in this native port and its seven AsyncRT regression files remain
  unqualified.
* Build configurations: the optimized build lacks LLVM assertion-only checks.
  A full assertions/debug build and full ASan/UBSan/TSan configurations remain
  unqualified. Selected generated-code ASan crash tests already pass, which is
  narrower coverage. Foreign-OS tests should stay foreign; cross-target and
  aarch64 execution need their own target qualification.
* GPU/MAX: three stdlib GPU targets are explicitly excluded; range codegen is
  incompatible with the source-built compiler. MAX layout integration, C-host
  parallelism and profiling-context tests need MAX libraries. The Radeon 780M
  has a working graphics driver, but native Mojo GPU execution/compiler backends
  and ROCm/HIP are not qualified. Graphics/Vulkan is not a HIP test result.
* Packaging: native Bazel and JDK packages, staged Mojo package QA and
  separately unpacked examples/Python/LSP checks pass. The fresh complete
  ports build and extracted-package checks now pass; clean Mojo VM lifecycle
  qualification remains pending. The Bazel/JDK VM lifecycle now passes. The first VM
  retry passed installation/dependency/file checks but hit its 30-minute wall
  limit during example compilation; this is not a lifecycle pass.

Evidence
--------
Original baseline: mojo-cpu-verified-tests.log, cpu-complete-audit.json.
Inventory: test-inventory.json (generated rule metadata; includes duplicate
aggregate/per-file and manual checks).
Audit attempts: mojo-coverage-native-tests.log, mojo-coverage-expanded-tests.log.
The first broad run had 43 passes and three failures; it also had Python
analysis errors. Preserve these failures alongside the corrected final run.

AI capability boundary
----------------------
This port currently establishes native CPU compilation and Python integration,
not an operational MAX model server or native GPU kernel execution. The host
Radeon 780M is on Modular's known-compatible hardware list for supported
systems, but this machine lacks the required native HIP runtime. GPU compiler
components, vendor runtime integration and MAX libraries require separate
qualification. A vector-add/memory-copy test and then a model generating tokens
are necessary milestones before claiming GPU or language-model support.
Upstream references checked 2026-10-09:
 https://mojolang.org/docs/requirements/
 https://max.modular.com/stable/api/mojo/

A separate inference route already exists in this ports checkout:
 misc/llama-cpp, using misc/ggml (VULKAN is a default ggml option)
 misc/ollama (CPU and VULKAN are default backend options)
These use a different backend path from Mojo/MAX. A separate llama.cpp development build has now run SmolLM2 on the CPU.
One Vulkan generation and 101 ADD checks passed, but a repeat run failed with
GPU device loss. Further GPU tests are deferred by the user. See
README.mojo-hardware.5BSD for evidence and the separate Mojo/MAX limitations.
No inference port was installed.

Package archive verification update (2026-10-09)
-----------------------------------------------
An initial repackaging invocation discarded generated plist entries, omitting
Mojo examples and most JDK files. The procedure now restages and packages
without deleting that generated list. The corrected archives match every
staged file: Mojo 26, JDK 519, Python 8029 and Bazel 5. JDK Java registration
hooks are present. Corrected Mojo examples, CPU counts and both ASan diagnostic
modes pass. Evidence: package-file-coverage.json, language-tools-package/ and
openjdk21-complete-manifest-package.log under the validation directory.
The earlier incomplete archive hashes are retained as failure evidence.

Two-domain VM and dependency validation (2026-10-09)
--------------------------------------------------
The corrected isolated VM fixture now passes all 40 BaseTest and 18 WorkQueue
cases with vm.ndomains=2, including native affinity save/restore. This extends
the single-domain host result; it does not qualify physical multi-socket memory
placement or PCI locality. Packaged example/lifecycle checks remain incomplete;
the synthetic QEMU max CPU failed code generation, and a named CPU retry is running.
The initial VM failure was the fixture's executable path; the unchanged test
passes with its original Support/unittests/BaseTest layout restored.

The Bazel/JDK package audit found GLib selecting ports elfutils despite its
base ELF headers. devel/glib20 revision 1 now selects base libelf consistently.
A compiled ELF resource passes listing and byte-for-byte extraction, and its
DT_NEEDED selects libelf.so.2. Evidence: glib-base-elf/result.json and
jdk-runtime-dependencies/devel-glib20-base-libelf.log. Offline dependency and
VM qualification follow separately; these checks do not install host packages.

Formatter CPU coverage (2026-10-09)
----------------------------------
The complete direct upstream pytest suite now passes 1004 cases, with two
upstream skips and zero failures. The 18 previously unconditional filesystem
skips now run outside Bazel. Current Click needs test-helper API updates;
the source archive also omitted six discovery fixture files and retained two
pre-Mojo .py ignore patterns. These are repaired as separate port test patches.
The two config-discovery mocks now exercise real temporary project configs.
The remaining skips are an obsolete Click API and a Python-stub-specific case. Optional compiler-validation tests retain their
four upstream file exclusions and are still running. This count is separate
from the 1179 Bazel targets qualified across earlier runs.
Evidence: formatter/unit-tests.{log,xml,json}; failed attempts remain alongside.

Latest dependency and package qualification (2026-10-09, 20:30 UTC)
----------------------------------------------------------------
The fresh 64-file Mojo package passes extracted-package examples, CPU-count and
ASan diagnostics, language-server protocol/Python checks and formatting checks.
It excludes the withdrawn llama.cpp bridge. The older Haswell VM run completed
operators and process examples but timed out at 7200 seconds during Python
compilation; it is not a completed lifecycle pass. A new disposable VM checks
the fresh package with a small SIMD compile/run, formatter, language-server
startup, Python module imports, removal and reinstallation. This narrower VM
check does not replace the physical host's larger examples/Python suite.

The Bazel/JDK retry passes offline native C++/Java/Python actions and package
removal/reinstallation. Its Bash parser failure came from using byacc while
skipping the declared Bison build dependency. Rebuilding with Bison fixes it;
all five upstream command-substitution/parser regression scripts pass. The
incorrect private Bash build is quarantined outside the ports work directory.
No new Bash source patch or host installation was needed.

LLDB/mojo-full now builds. The debugger API suite aborts while loading the Mojo
plugin due to duplicate LLVM command-line registration. A development-only
linker visibility fix is queued after baseline debugger/Jupyter checks. It is
not in the packaged port and is not yet qualified.

Evidence: fresh-complete-package/checks.json, bash-parser-tests/result.json,
/usr/obj/mojo-bazel-jdk-vm/bison-bash-retry-result.json,
/usr/obj/mojo-qualification-vm/language-tools-haswell-result.json,
/usr/obj/mojo-fresh-qualification-vm/result.json.

Pending CPU qualification follow-up (2026-10-09)
-----------------------------------------------
The first fresh 64-file package VM reached the formatter but the guest test
used an unsupported `mojo format --check` option. That test-script error is
preserved under mojo-fresh-qualification-vm/failed-unsupported-check-option/.
The corrected test compares the result of two formatting passes, and retries
on a fresh disk overlay with the identical package. Lifecycle remains pending.
An assertions-enabled full CPU suite is queued in /usr/obj/mojo-assertions-output
using dbg mode with O2 and LLVM ABI breaking checks. It waits for the current
debugger prototype and VM checks; queued is not a passing test result.
